      architecture: {
        id: 'architecture',
        title: 'Setup & Architecture',
        content:
          "**The physical setup is simple once you see the flow — and one specific placement detail (USB 3.0 interference) is the single most common avoidable mistake.**",
        items: [
          "Zigbee path: Mini PC/Pi (Home Assistant) → USB extension cable (1–2m) → Zigbee coordinator dongle → Zigbee mesh (routers + end devices, e.g. bulbs and plugs act as repeaters) → your sensors, bulbs, and plugs.",
          "Thread path: Thread border router (built into an existing smart speaker, or the ZBT-2/Dongle Plus MG24 in Thread mode) → Thread mesh → Matter-over-Thread devices.",
          "Don't plug the coordinator directly into the mini PC's own USB port. SONOFF's own documentation, and community reports on the ZBT-2, both flag that a mini PC's USB 3.0 ports and internal Wi-Fi radio can interfere with the 2.4GHz band Zigbee and Thread both use — a basic USB extension cable moving the dongle 1–2 meters away is real, well-documented advice, not a marketing add-on.",
          "Avoid USB hubs for the radio dongle specifically — a hub can add latency that a direct connection (via the extension cable) doesn't have.",
          "Some coordinators need a one-time firmware flash before first use to run the exact firmware your chosen integration (ZHA or Zigbee2MQTT) expects — check the specific product's current setup documentation rather than assuming it arrives pre-flashed for your integration.",
        ],
        affiliateLinks: [
          { label: 'Check current price — USB 2.0 extension cable', url: 'https://www.amazon.com/s?k=USB+2.0+extension+cable+1m', productName: 'USB extension cable (1–2m)', productCategory: 'Accessory', priceRange: '$5–10' },
        ],
      },
      howManyDevices: {
        id: 'how-many-devices',
        title: 'How Many Devices Can One Coordinator Support?',
        content:
          "**Don't buy based on a single marketing device-count number — actual capacity depends on the coordinator, how many powered \"router\" devices are in your mesh, and physical placement, not the dongle alone.**",
        items: [
          "Zigbee is a mesh network: mains-powered devices (bulbs, plugs, many in-wall switches) act as routers that relay signal and extend range, while battery devices (most sensors) are end devices that only talk to the nearest router — more powered devices generally means a stronger, larger-capacity mesh, not just more strain on the coordinator.",
          "The coordinator itself has a direct-child limit that's real but is not the whole story — Zigbee2MQTT's and Home Assistant's own documentation both frame practical network size as a function of mesh health (router density and placement), not a single number you can shop by.",
          "If you're planning a large device count (100+), prioritize router-capable devices spread through the house over trusting one coordinator's spec sheet number — a dense mesh with an ordinary coordinator will outperform a high-spec coordinator with a thin mesh.",
          "This is also a real advantage of the Ethernet/PoE SMLIGHT option for large houses: central placement plus a healthy router mesh does more for reliable coverage than any single coordinator's on-paper device limit.",
        ],
      },
